%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e9620f8c34305754d8cc9a7e49e852d9, type: 3}
  m_Name: info_Table_en
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_LocaleId:
    m_Code: en
  m_SharedData: {fileID: 11400000, guid: e6909cd5edeaea74aab924612974dffc, type: 2}
  m_Metadata:
    m_Items: []
  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 300936355840
    m_Localized: History
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 301066379264
    m_Localized: "The Casas Reales (Royal Houses)\u2014headquarters of the Real Audiencia
      of Quito, established by the Spanish Crown in 1563\u2014were settled on this
      site in 1612. By the mid-18th century, the original structures were demolished;
      subsequently, adjacent properties were annexed to construct a new edifice atop
      a raised atrium with a stone balustrade, spanning the entire frontage of the
      square.\n\nFollowing the independence era, the building became the seat of
      the Republican Government. Between 1956 and 1960, during the administration
      of President Camilo Ponce Enr\xEDquez, the palace underwent an extensive reconstruction.
      Since the 1970s, it has been known as the <i>Carondelet</i> Palace, named in
      honor of Luis Francisco H\xE9ctor, Baron of Carondelet, who presided over the
      Royal Audience and governed from 1799 to 1807."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 301066379265
    m_Localized: Facade
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 301066379266
    m_Localized: "The current neoclassical facade originated with the building\u2019s
      Republican transformation, initiated in 1843 by the French architect Teodoro
      Lavezzari. During the administration of Jos\xE9 Mar\xEDa Urvina (1851\u20131856),
      the national coat of arms was integrated into the tympana of the lateral sections."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 301070573568
    m_Localized: Covachas (Vaulted Street-Level Stalls)
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 301070573569
    m_Localized: "A dozen <i>covachas</i>\u2014small, vaulted commercial spaces\u2014are
      set into the building's massive foundation."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 301070573570
    m_Localized: Andesite Stone
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 301070573571
    m_Localized: Certain blocks of andesite stone within the foundation exhibit polygonal
      masonry, reminiscent of the stonework found in Incaic structures. This architectural
      resemblance has led to unfounded speculation regarding their pre-Hispanic origins;
      however, such claims remain unsupported by archaeological evidence.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 301070573572
    m_Localized: The Blue Shield (Blue Emblem)
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 301070573573
    m_Localized: "Installed on April 5, 2024, during the administration of President
      Daniel Noboa Az\xEDn, this emblem is located at the intersection of Chile and
      Garc\xEDa Moreno streets. This distinctive marker identifies the building as
      a protected cultural property under the 1954 Hague Convention, serving to safeguard
      the site in the event of internal or external armed conflict."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 301070573574
    m_Localized: Colonnade
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 301070573575
    m_Localized: The series of Tuscan-style stone columns was installed in 1960,
      replacing the original brick structures erected in 1845.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 301070573576
    m_Localized: "Abd\xF3n Calder\xF3n Commemorative Plaque"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 301070573577
    m_Localized: "The commemorative plaque honors the ultimate sacrifice of the \"Boy
      Hero\", Abd\xF3n Calder\xF3n, during the Battle of Pichincha on May 24, 1822."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 301070573578
    m_Localized: Commemorative Tablets
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966259765248
    m_Localized: The commemorative tablets or plaques record the establishment of
      the <i>Real Audiencia</i> at this site in 1612 (lower tablet) and the reconstruction
      of the palace in 1747 (upper tablet).
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966339457024
    m_Localized: "Sim\xF3n Bol\xEDvar Commemorative Plaque"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966339457025
    m_Localized: "The commemorative plaque marks the arrival of The Liberator, Sim\xF3n
      Bol\xEDvar, in Quito on June 16, 1822."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966339457026
    m_Localized: Wrought Iron Balustrade
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966339457027
    m_Localized: "It is widely presumed that Antonio Flores Jij\xF3n, serving as
      the Ecuadorian Minister to France, acquired the iron railings from the demolished
      <i>Tuileries</i> Palace during an auction in Paris. Today, these historic gates
      are integrated into the palace, connecting several columns along the atrium."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966339457028
    m_Localized: Granaderos de Tarqui (Presidential Guard)
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966339457029
    m_Localized: The full dress uniform of the presidential guard is a historical
      recreation of the attire worn by the <i>Granaderos de Tarqui</i>. This battalion
      fought in the namesake battle of 1829, which resulted in the defeat of the
      Peruvian forces that had invaded the southern territories of Colombia.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651328
    m_Localized: "<b>Gabriel Garc\xEDa Moreno Commemorative Tablets</b>"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651329
    m_Localized: "<b>Photo 1:</b> A commemorative tablet marking the exact site of
      the assassination of President Gabriel Garc\xEDa Moreno on August 6, 1875.\n\n<b>Photo
      2:</b> A memorial plaque dedicated to President Gabriel Garc\xEDa Moreno, installed
      by his political associates (co-ideologues) to commemorate the 90th anniversary
      of his death."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651330
    m_Localized: <b>Tablet Commemorating the Centennial of the Spanish Capitulation,
      after the Battle of Pichincha</b>
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651331
    m_Localized: 'The commemorative tablet marks the signing of the Spanish act of
      capitulation following the Battle of Pichincha on May 24, 1822. The inscription
      reads: "<i>In this location, on May 25, 1822, the act of capitulation mandated
      by the victory at Pichincha was signed. The Tailors'' Society. Quito, May 1922.</i>"'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651332
    m_Localized: The Baron of Carondelet
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651333
    m_Localized: As President, the Baron of Carondelet was distinguished by his commitment
      to the progress and development of <i>the Real Audiencia of Quito</i>. This
      painting is a modern copy of the original portrait housed in the Quito Cathedral.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651334
    m_Localized: Fountains
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651335
    m_Localized: "The reconstruction during the administration of President Camilo
      Ponce Enr\xEDquez was exhaustive. Two symmetrical courtyards were created,
      featuring cloisters, arcades, and stone fountains carved by Ignacio Codena."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651336
    m_Localized: National Flag
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651337
    m_Localized: As a sign of respect, visitors traditionally offer a bow in front
      of the National Flag
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651338
    m_Localized: Mural "Discovery of the Amazon River"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651339
    m_Localized: "Presented as a triptych in Venetian tile mosaic, the work narrates
      the epic discovery of the Amazon River. The expedition began in Quito with
      approximately 200 Spaniards led by Gonzalo Pizarro and over 3,000 indigenous
      people taken by force. They crossed the eastern Andes and entered the dense
      rainforest.\nOn February 12, 1542, Captain Francisco de Orellana, separated
      from the main expedition, reached the <i>R\xEDo Mar</i>. Unable to return,
      he navigated to its mouth in the Atlantic to report the discovery to the Spanish
      Crown. \nIt was named the Amazon River because, during the journey, they were
      allegedly attacked by a tribe of women warriors who reminded them of the Amazons
      of Greek mythology."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651340
    m_Localized: Piano
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651341
    m_Localized: A baby grand piano, Kawai brand, donated by the Embassy of China
      to Ecuador in February 2022.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651342
    m_Localized: "<b>Sim\xF3n Bol\xEDvar</b>"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651343
    m_Localized: 'Marco Salas

      Oil on canvas

      XX century'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651344
    m_Localized: "<b>Antonio Jos\xE9 de Sucre</b>"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651345
    m_Localized: 'Victor Mideros

      Oil on canvas

      XX century'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651346
    m_Localized: <b>Eugenio Espejo</b>
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651347
    m_Localized: 'Oswaldo Viteri

      Oil on canvas

      XX century'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651348
    m_Localized: "<b>Manuela S\xE1enz</b>"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651349
    m_Localized: 'Oswaldo Viteri

      Oil on canvas

      XX century'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651350
    m_Localized: "Replica of the Antonio Jos\xE9 de Sucre Sculpture"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651351
    m_Localized: "The bronze replica was created by French artist Emmanuele Fontaine.
      Beyond its artistic value, it bears a historical mark: during the 1975 attempted
      coup to overthrow General Guillermo Rodr\xEDguez Lara, the sculpture was struck
      by a projectile."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651352
    m_Localized: ' Five pre-Hispanic pieces'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651353
    m_Localized: "Ornamental Jar \nThree Ornamental plates \nTripod pot\n"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651354
    m_Localized: Executive Desk
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651355
    m_Localized: "Crafted by artisan Neptal\xED Mart\xEDnez Jaramillo. In 1986, President
      Le\xF3n Febres Cordero honored him with the <i>Order of Merit</i> (Officer
      Grade) for his notable contribution to the development of artistic craftsmanship
      in Ecuador and abroad."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651356
    m_Localized: Coffered Ceiling <i>(Artesonado)</i>
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651357
    m_Localized: "The coffered ceiling was crafted from cedar wood in the 1950s by
      Miguel \xC1ngel and Leonardo Tejada. The gilding\u2014a decorative technique
      involving the application of gold leaf\u2014was performed by Luis Villag\xF3mez."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651358
    m_Localized: Furniture
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651359
    m_Localized: "The armchairs, tables, and consoles with marble-topped mirrors
      were commissioned from the workshop of Neptal\xED Mart\xEDnez Jaramillo."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651360
    m_Localized: French Console
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651361
    m_Localized: A gilded French console featuring rock crystal mirrors.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651362
    m_Localized: Iranian Vases
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651363
    m_Localized: Made of copper, hand-painted and enameled in Isfahan, Iran, using
      the ancient Minakari technique.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651364
    m_Localized: Korean Vases
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651365
    m_Localized: "Reproduction of 12th-century porcelain from the Goryeo Dynasty
      (918\u20131392)."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651366
    m_Localized: 'Tibor (Ginger Jar): '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651367
    m_Localized: The blue and green porcelain lidded jar, notable for its chalice-shaped
      form, is distinguished by its delicate ornamentation. The front features a
      landscape depicting figures adorned in traditional Chinese allegorical attire.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651368
    m_Localized: Bronze Lamp
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651369
    m_Localized: Represents Libertas (the Roman goddess of Liberty). A full-length
      sculpture in cast, polished, and chiseled bronze. Anonymous author, 20th century.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651370
    m_Localized: Cabinet Room Furniture
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651371
    m_Localized: "The set consists of a large wooden table and centennial chairs
      attributed to the Italian artist Juan Bautista Minghetti. \nThe pieces were
      reportedly carved at the School of Arts and Crafts (<i>Escuela de Artes y Oficios</i>)
      run by the Salesian Fathers. Before arriving at the Palace, they were used
      in the legislative chambers. The President's chair, located at the head of
      the table, is carved with the National Coat of Arms."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651372
    m_Localized: 'Coffered Ceiling (<i>Artesonado</i>) '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651373
    m_Localized: "The cedar wood coffered ceiling was executed by Ra\xFAl Robalino.
      Suspended from it are two large crystal chandeliers, originally from the legislative
      chambers, which were meticulously restored and modernized in 1960."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651374
    m_Localized: "Wainscoting (<i>Z\xF3calo</i>) "
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651375
    m_Localized: The wooden wainscoting was crafted by the artisan Vicente Arboleda
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651376
    m_Localized: 'Tapestry '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651377
    m_Localized: The tapestry covering the walls was woven in Guangopolo, a parish
      located in the outskirts of Quito.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651378
    m_Localized: <b>Madonna and child </b>
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651379
    m_Localized: "Anonymous \nOil on canvas \n17th Century \nNational Museum Collection,
      Ministry of Education, Sports, and Culture."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651380
    m_Localized: 'Coffered Ceiling (<i>Artesonado</i>) '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651381
    m_Localized: "The coffered ceiling is the work of Miguel \xC1ngel Tejada."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651382
    m_Localized: Furniture
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651383
    m_Localized: "The furniture was made by Salom\xF3n Enr\xEDquez and V\xEDctor
      Mogoll\xF3n as part of the 1960 palace remodeling."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651384
    m_Localized: "Wainscoting (<i>Z\xF3calo</i>) "
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651385
    m_Localized: "A wide and elevated wooden wainscoting surrounds the hall. It serves
      as a base for the display of various artworks from several of the country\u2019s
      heritage repositories."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651386
    m_Localized: <b>Pedro Vicente Maldonado (1704-1748)</b>
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651387
    m_Localized: "Enrique G\xF3mezjurado \nYear 1936 \nOil on canvas \nAlberto Mena
      Caama\xF1o Reserve Collection, Quito-Ecuador\n\nGeographer and cartographer
      born in Riobamba and deceased in London. Author of the route from Quito to
      Esmeraldas and the \"<i>Carta de la Provincia de Quito y de sus adyacentes</i>\"
      (Map of the Province of Quito and its Adjacents), printed in Paris in 1750
      by his friend and mentor, Charles Marie de La Condamine. He was a member of
      the French Academy of Sciences and the Royal Geographical Society of London."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651388
    m_Localized: "<b>Francisco Jos\xE9 de Caldas (1768-1816)</b>"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651389
    m_Localized: "Anonymous \n20th Century \nOil on canvas \nAlberto Mena Caama\xF1o
      Reserve Collection, Quito-Ecuador \n\nColombian geographer, astronomer, naturalist,
      and patriot. He conducted extensive research on flora, establishing ties with
      Jos\xE9 Celestino Mutis, as well as Humboldt and Bonpland, whom he met in Quito.
      His experiences were chronicled in \"<i>Viaje: de Quito a las costas del Oc\xE9ano
      Pac\xEDfico</i>\" (1803) and \"<i>Viaje al coraz\xF3n de Barnuevo</i>\" (Insights
      on his trips around Quito)."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651390
    m_Localized: <b>Eugenio Espejo (1747-1795)</b>
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651391
    m_Localized: "Murgueitio \n20th Century \nOil on canvas \nAlberto Mena Caama\xF1o
      Reserve Collection, Quito-Ecuador\n\nEnlightened physician, lawyer, and writer
      from Quito; a precursor to independence. His writings, the publication of \"<i>Primicias
      de la Cultura de Quito</i>\" (the first newspaper of the Audience in 1792),
      and the founding of the \"<i>Sociedad Patri\xF3tica de Amigos del Pa\xEDs de
      Quito</i>\" were instrumental in developing a national consciousness. Persecuted
      and imprisoned for sedition, he died in Quito in December 1795 following a
      rigorous incarceration."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651392
    m_Localized: "<b>Manuela Ca\xF1izares</b>"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651393
    m_Localized: "Anonymous \n19th Century \nOil on canvas \nAlberto Mena Caama\xF1o
      Reserve Collection, Quito-Ecuador\n\nWomen\u2019s participation was decisive
      in the emancipation process. They acted in diverse ways, sacrificing their
      peace, reputations, and assets\u2014whether abundant or meager\u2014even offering
      their lives. Do\xF1a Manuela Ca\xF1izares hosted one of the city's most active
      <i>tertulias</i> (intellectual gatherings) in her residence across from the
      Public University. Intellectuals and patriots met there, and it was from this
      location, in the early hours of August 10, 1809, that they acted to depose
      the Spanish authorities."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966343651394
    m_Localized: "<b>The Liberator Sim\xF3n Bol\xEDvar (1783\u20131830) </b>"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845632
    m_Localized: "V\xEDctor Mideros \n20th Century \nOil on canvas \n\nVenezuelan
      military and political leader who liberated the modern-day republics of Venezuela,
      Colombia, Panama, Ecuador, Peru, and Bolivia from Spanish rule. An undisputed
      leader and national hero, he remains a cultural icon throughout Latin America.
      In 1819, he founded the Republic of Colombia (Gran Colombia). He served as
      its president until 1830 when, disillusioned by internal divisions and the
      rise of local <i>caudillos</i>, he resigned. He died on December 17, 1830,
      while en route to exile."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845633
    m_Localized: "<b>Manuela S\xE1enz (1797-1856)</b>"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845634
    m_Localized: "Anonymous \n20th Century\nOil on canvas \nAlberto Mena Caama\xF1o
      Reserve Collection, Quito-Ecuador\n\nHeroine of Ecuadorian Independence and
      the companion of the Liberator Sim\xF3n Bol\xEDvar. She began her struggle
      for the American cause in Lima, where she was decorated by the Liberator San
      Mart\xEDn. She met Bol\xEDvar in Quito in June 1822, becoming his confidante
      and advisor. She famously saved his life on the night of September 28, 1828,
      earning the title \"The Liberatress of the Liberator.\""
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845635
    m_Localized: "<b>Antonio Jos\xE9 de Sucre (1795-1830)</b>"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845636
    m_Localized: "Emilio Moncayo\n20th Century \nOil on canvas \n\nVenezuelan military
      officer, statesman, and diplomat. He triumphed at the Battle of Pichincha on
      May 24, 1822, definitively securing the independence of the territory of the
      former Audience of Quito. At the Battle of Ayacucho (1824), he sealed the emancipation
      of Peru and was named Grand Marshal. After serving as the first president of
      Bolivia, he resigned in 1828 to settle in Quito with his wife, Mariana Carcel\xE9n.
      He was assassinated in the Berruecos mountains on June 4, 1830."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845637
    m_Localized: "<b>Juan Jos\xE9 Flores (1800-1864)</b>"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845638
    m_Localized: "Marco Salas \n20th Century \nOil on canvas \n\nA high-ranking Venezuelan
      officer of the independence army. Appointed by Bol\xEDvar as Intendant of the
      Southern Department of Colombia, he later became the First President of the
      Republic of Ecuador in 1830. During his second term (1843\u20131845), he promoted
      the reconstruction of the Government Palace in the Neoclassical style. He was
      overthrown by the Nationalist \"March Revolution\" in 1845. Later rehabilitated,
      he became a key ally to President Garc\xEDa Moreno"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845639
    m_Localized: <b>Vicente Rocafuerte (1783-1847)</b>
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845640
    m_Localized: "V\xEDctor Mideros \n20th Century \nOil on canvas \n\nA man of profound
      culture, he was educated in Europe from a young age. Elected as a deputy to
      the Courts of C\xE1diz, he later served as a diplomat for Mexico. As a deputy
      to the Ecuadorian Congress in 1833, he fiercely opposed General Flores. After
      the conflict subsided, he was elected the Second President of Ecuador, noted
      for his administrative work in education and the economy."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845641
    m_Localized: "<b>Gabriel Garc\xEDa Moreno (1821-1875)</b>"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845642
    m_Localized: "V\xEDctor Mideros\n20th Century \nOil on canvas \n\nPresident of
      the Republic for two terms (1861\u20131865; 1869\u20131875), he was the dominant
      figure in Ecuadorian politics of the era. He sought to implement a rigid Catholic
      morality to achieve national order and progress. He stabilized public finances,
      expanded education, and initiated the construction of the Guayaquil-Quito railway.His
      administrations were characterized by intolerance and repression, which garnered
      him numerous enemies; he was subsequently assassinated on August 6, 1875, shortly
      before commencing his third term in office.\nDuring his two terms in office,
      he undertook significant reconstruction efforts at the Government Palace following
      the earthquakes of 1859 and 1868."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845643
    m_Localized: "<b>Antonio Flores Jij\xF3n (1833-1915)</b>\n"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845644
    m_Localized: "C\xE9sar A. Villacr\xE9s\nYear 1917\nOil on canvas \nAlberto Mena
      Caama\xF1o Reserve Collection, Quito-Ecuador\n\nStatesman, diplomat, and academic.
      Son of the First President, Juan Jos\xE9 Flores. He served as President of
      the Republic between 1888 and 1892 with a progressive Catholic approach. With
      the advent of the Liberal regime in 1895, he went into self-exile in Europe.\n"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845645
    m_Localized: <b>Encampment after the Earthquake in Quito</b>
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845646
    m_Localized: "Anonymous \n20th Century \nOil on canvas \nAlberto Mena Caama\xF1o
      Reserve Collection, Quito-Ecuador\n\nOn August 16, 1868, a violent earthquake
      destroyed Ibarra and other northern towns, resulting in at least 15,000 deaths.
      Although the seismic force was lower in Quito, it caused significant damage
      to bell towers, vaults, and the Government Palace. Terrified by aftershocks,
      the population improvised housing in public squares to avoid being caught in
      collapsing structures while asleep."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845647
    m_Localized: 'Uruguayan Amethyst '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845648
    m_Localized: The amethyst is a stone of intense violet color, one of the highest
      quality in the world.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845649
    m_Localized: Vase
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845650
    m_Localized: Gift from Li Ruogu, Chairman of Exim Bank.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845651
    m_Localized: Furniture
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845652
    m_Localized: "Anonymous \n20th Century\nA high, narrow mahogany table in the
      Neo-Egyptian Empire style, belonging to the old Government Palace. It features
      gilded metal appliqu\xE9s depicting sphinxes and winged lions."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845653
    m_Localized: 'Sideboard (<i>Aparador</i>) '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845654
    m_Localized: "Anonymous \n20th Century \nThis wooden sideboard is notable for
      its gilded metal appliqu\xE9s and moldings. Its columns are decorated with
      a helical floral garland"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845655
    m_Localized: 'Limoges Fine Bone China '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845656
    m_Localized: "A porcelain plate and tureen commissioned from the House of Limoges,
      France, by Mercedes Jij\xF3n, wife of President Juan Jos\xE9 Flores, during
      his first term (1830\u20131834). \nThey feature the first Coat of Arms of the
      Republic: the peaks of <i>La Marca</i> and a rising sun. However, the production
      contains an error; pheasants were painted atop the peaks instead of eagles."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845657
    m_Localized: <b>Presidential Tableware</b>
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845658
    m_Localized: "Since 1960, the Palace has maintained an elegant collection of
      European china, glassware, and silver acquired from the houses of <i>Purhardt</i>
      (England), <i>Christofle and Baccarat</i> (France), and <i>Ascen\xE7ao</i>
      (Portugal). Many pieces bear the official 1860 Coat of Arms of Ecuador.\n\"Many
      of these pieces bear the official Ecuadorian Coat of Arms of 1860, serving
      as enduring testaments to republican history and protocol.\""
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845659
    m_Localized: 'Altarpiece (<i>Retablo</i>) '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845660
    m_Localized: A small, carved, and gilded wooden altarpiece featuring 18th-century
      polychrome sculptures of the Immaculate Conception, Saint Joachim, and Saint
      Anne, from the National Museum of Ecuador.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845661
    m_Localized: 'Virgin of the Immaculate Conception '
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 6903966347845662
